WEBVTT

00:00.250 --> 00:02.490
Instructor: So we just saw git restore.

00:02.490 --> 00:04.980
Now we're moving on to a similar sounding command,

00:04.980 --> 00:06.690
which is git reset.

00:06.690 --> 00:09.360
The git reset command will reset a repository

00:09.360 --> 00:11.880
back to some particular commit.

00:11.880 --> 00:14.730
So if we're working on, let's say the master branch

00:14.730 --> 00:17.490
and then I realized that the last three commits

00:17.490 --> 00:19.290
that I've made we're not supposed to be

00:19.290 --> 00:20.123
on the master branch.

00:20.123 --> 00:21.450
I screwed that up and I wanted to put them

00:21.450 --> 00:24.390
on a different branch and I want to remove them from master.

00:24.390 --> 00:25.470
I can do that.

00:25.470 --> 00:28.230
I undo those commits using git reset.

00:28.230 --> 00:30.900
And there's two flavors, two varieties I'll show you.

00:30.900 --> 00:34.470
There's regular reset and then a hard reset.

00:34.470 --> 00:35.940
And we're gonna start with I guess

00:35.940 --> 00:37.530
the plain old regular reset.

00:37.530 --> 00:38.700
I don't know if anyone calls it that

00:38.700 --> 00:41.850
but that's what I'll call it, plain reset.

00:41.850 --> 00:42.690
It looks like this:

00:42.690 --> 00:44.970
git reset and then a commit hash.

00:44.970 --> 00:46.590
And this will reset our repository

00:46.590 --> 00:48.810
back to whatever that commit hash is.

00:48.810 --> 00:53.340
Let me demonstrate this with our really boring cat, dog.

00:53.340 --> 00:55.260
First, second, third, fourth commit.

00:55.260 --> 00:58.260
I'm gonna go, let's delete this secrets file

00:58.260 --> 01:00.420
just to make our lives easier.

01:00.420 --> 01:02.520
Okay, so it's gone.

01:02.520 --> 01:06.060
Let's make a mistake or do something in here

01:06.060 --> 01:07.800
that we just want to undo.

01:07.800 --> 01:09.450
Let's say it's that.

01:09.450 --> 01:11.913
And then this one over here I delete that,

01:12.750 --> 01:15.030
and then I'm gonna save this

01:15.030 --> 01:17.850
and then I'm gonna make a commit accidentally,

01:17.850 --> 01:19.110
which I'm doing on purpose

01:19.110 --> 01:21.810
but we'll pretend I don't actually want this commit.

01:21.810 --> 01:25.380
git commit -m mistake commit.

01:25.380 --> 01:27.360
Okay, so here's what we have now.

01:27.360 --> 01:30.420
First, second, third, fourth, and then mistake commit.

01:30.420 --> 01:33.480
And you can see, you know, I added those two lines

01:33.480 --> 01:36.330
and then I deleted this in the dog file.

01:36.330 --> 01:38.280
Anyway, we've screwed it up.

01:38.280 --> 01:40.230
And maybe I do one more commit,

01:40.230 --> 01:42.030
that's even a a further mistake.

01:42.030 --> 01:44.883
Why don't I, I don't know, add a happy face.

01:46.440 --> 01:47.523
Same thing over here.

01:48.420 --> 01:49.253
git add .

01:50.520 --> 01:52.140
git commit -m.

01:52.140 --> 01:54.993
Another mistake or another bad commit.

01:55.950 --> 01:59.850
Okay, so now I can use the git reset command.

01:59.850 --> 02:02.220
If I find a commit, I want to go back to,

02:02.220 --> 02:04.593
so git log, let's just do one line.

02:05.520 --> 02:07.920
If I want to go back to fourth commit

02:07.920 --> 02:10.500
I can reference this particular commit hash,

02:10.500 --> 02:12.510
or this is the first seven digits.

02:12.510 --> 02:13.770
Copy that.

02:13.770 --> 02:18.063
And then git reset and that hash just like this.

02:19.110 --> 02:21.120
And what happened?

02:21.120 --> 02:24.210
Well, you might think those changes are gone

02:24.210 --> 02:25.920
but they're actually not.

02:25.920 --> 02:27.663
Those changes are still here.

02:29.040 --> 02:31.110
Doesn't that seem like they should be reset?

02:31.110 --> 02:34.260
No, because all that we did was perform a basic

02:34.260 --> 02:37.950
or a plain reset and that removed the commits

02:37.950 --> 02:41.490
but the changes are still in our working directory.

02:41.490 --> 02:43.140
So I can get rid of those changes

02:43.140 --> 02:46.410
and I can, you know, undo stuff in my local files.

02:46.410 --> 02:49.380
But all that we did right now was tell Git

02:49.380 --> 02:51.540
to get rid of some commits.

02:51.540 --> 02:56.430
Go back to this last commit that, what was it, 4661.

02:56.430 --> 02:59.700
So in Gits mind, that's where the history stops.

02:59.700 --> 03:01.590
There is no commit after that, right?

03:01.590 --> 03:04.680
git log, just do one line again.

03:04.680 --> 03:06.600
That's the last commit it has.

03:06.600 --> 03:09.420
But it kept those changes in our working directory.

03:09.420 --> 03:11.940
So here's a diagram to show that.

03:11.940 --> 03:13.980
Let's say we make a commit and we realize,

03:13.980 --> 03:16.320
ah, we don't want that commit.

03:16.320 --> 03:19.260
So to undo it, if we use git reset

03:19.260 --> 03:21.630
and a commit hash to go back to this one,

03:21.630 --> 03:24.120
those changes are still in our working directory.

03:24.120 --> 03:27.360
We don't lose the changes, we lose the commits.

03:27.360 --> 03:30.210
However many commits there were between head,

03:30.210 --> 03:32.160
all the way back to this commit.

03:32.160 --> 03:33.930
It just happened to be one.

03:33.930 --> 03:38.580
So in our case, we undid two commits over here, right?

03:38.580 --> 03:40.860
There were two commits, one was mistake

03:40.860 --> 03:43.170
and the second was called bad commit I think.

03:43.170 --> 03:45.030
But we still have the changes.

03:45.030 --> 03:45.863
This can be useful

03:45.863 --> 03:47.880
if you make some commits on the wrong branch.

03:47.880 --> 03:51.300
You wanna keep that work but move it to another branch.

03:51.300 --> 03:52.710
So we've reset, right?

03:52.710 --> 03:55.320
We lost those commits on the master branch

03:55.320 --> 03:57.420
but now I could take these changes with me

03:57.420 --> 04:00.120
and make a new branch, git switch,

04:00.120 --> 04:04.437
switch -c, you know, I dunno, badstuff.

04:05.847 --> 04:06.823
And on here I can add and commit those changes.

04:08.912 --> 04:10.445
git add .

04:10.445 --> 04:12.612
git commit, add bad stuff.

04:16.590 --> 04:17.751
I dunno, it's stupid.

04:17.751 --> 04:21.180
So now I have this branch that I've made that has, you know,

04:21.180 --> 04:24.990
these mistakes, the stuff that I presumably don't want.

04:24.990 --> 04:28.440
And now I can go back to master

04:28.440 --> 04:31.440
and things are how we want them to be on master.

04:31.440 --> 04:34.500
So I took those changes with me over to the new branch.

04:34.500 --> 04:36.720
I added and committed them on the new branch

04:36.720 --> 04:39.960
and then went back to master without those changes.

04:39.960 --> 04:41.790
Okay, so hopefully that was clear.

04:41.790 --> 04:44.940
Reset, git reset with a commit hash

04:44.940 --> 04:48.450
removes the commits, but it does not remove the changes

04:48.450 --> 04:49.620
from your working directory.

04:49.620 --> 04:53.070
So you don't lose that work, you lose the commits.

04:53.070 --> 04:57.360
The alternative is a hard reset, which is the same command

04:57.360 --> 04:59.490
but with --hard.

04:59.490 --> 05:03.210
And when we do a get reset --hard with a commit hash,

05:03.210 --> 05:06.480
what will actually happen is that we lose the commits,

05:06.480 --> 05:10.290
Git undoes the commits up to this commit hash

05:10.290 --> 05:13.860
and the changes are removed from our working directory.

05:13.860 --> 05:16.020
So if you like absolutely know

05:16.020 --> 05:17.580
you want to get rid of some work

05:17.580 --> 05:19.500
and get rid of the corresponding commits,

05:19.500 --> 05:21.420
you can use --hard.

05:21.420 --> 05:22.770
So you can reference the commit hash

05:22.770 --> 05:24.630
or you can do things like, you know,

05:24.630 --> 05:27.210
we've already seen HEAD~1 or ~2

05:27.210 --> 05:30.660
to go back relative to where head is.

05:30.660 --> 05:33.150
So on a diagram, if I wanted to undo this

05:33.150 --> 05:37.350
and get rid of those changes, git reset --hard,

05:37.350 --> 05:39.150
the commit hash that we're going back to,

05:39.150 --> 05:41.550
those changes are gone.

05:41.550 --> 05:44.220
So I'll do a quick demo of that.

05:44.220 --> 05:48.000
Let's go back, I guess let's go back to master.

05:48.000 --> 05:50.040
git switch master.

05:50.040 --> 05:52.740
And let's make another bad change.

05:52.740 --> 05:56.400
So we'll delete two of those and dog,

05:56.400 --> 05:58.850
and then we'll add a bunch of stuff in cat again.

06:00.180 --> 06:02.580
And then I'm gonna add and commit that on master.

06:02.580 --> 06:06.363
git commit, undo this commit please.

06:07.530 --> 06:10.710
Okay, so a regular git reset, as we saw,

06:10.710 --> 06:13.350
would keep these changes in our directory

06:13.350 --> 06:15.150
but it would remove commit.

06:15.150 --> 06:17.670
I am going to undo that last commit.

06:17.670 --> 06:21.300
So git log, let's just get the commit hash.

06:21.300 --> 06:25.080
I'll go back, maybe I'll go back to this one, third commit.

06:25.080 --> 06:28.560
So that means I will lose this commit and this commit,

06:28.560 --> 06:30.990
and I'll lose the the corresponding changes

06:30.990 --> 06:32.790
that I made in both of them.

06:32.790 --> 06:35.760
So you wanna be careful with this, obviously. (chuckles)

06:35.760 --> 06:38.710
You don't wanna go around just resetting hard all the time.

06:39.720 --> 06:40.553
But I'm gonna do it here.

06:40.553 --> 06:44.910
So git reset --hard commit hash.

06:44.910 --> 06:46.230
One more time, this is what we're looking at.

06:46.230 --> 06:47.760
Undo this commit please.

06:47.760 --> 06:50.880
On master fourth, commit third, second, and so on.

06:50.880 --> 06:52.350
Here we go.

06:52.350 --> 06:56.480
Okay, it says head is now at this third commit.

06:56.480 --> 06:59.250
As you can see, here's master.

06:59.250 --> 07:01.440
We no longer have that commit on master branch.

07:01.440 --> 07:03.870
We lost two commits on the master branch.

07:03.870 --> 07:05.790
One line.

07:05.790 --> 07:07.260
Now we just have three.

07:07.260 --> 07:10.860
And in my files they both are reset back

07:10.860 --> 07:11.910
to what they looked like.

07:11.910 --> 07:13.830
They have the same contents that they did

07:13.830 --> 07:17.220
when we made this third commit, we reset them back.

07:17.220 --> 07:20.820
Okay, so that's kind of all there is for that.

07:20.820 --> 07:22.500
I mean, I guess I should mention.

07:22.500 --> 07:24.030
This is on a per branch basis.

07:24.030 --> 07:26.700
So if we go back to my diagram,

07:26.700 --> 07:29.490
we still have that fourth commit over on bad stuff.

07:29.490 --> 07:32.730
It's not like we remove this commit from every branch ever

07:32.730 --> 07:34.920
that knows about that commit.

07:34.920 --> 07:35.910
It's still there.

07:35.910 --> 07:38.913
If I go to bad stuff, git switch badstuff,

07:39.840 --> 07:42.750
do a git log, we still see fourth commit.

07:42.750 --> 07:45.513
Anyway, so that's reset and reset hard.
